nodecode-phone-autopilot is a rewrite of jupist's jupist/phone-autopilot:

  https://github.com/jupist/phone-autopilot
  commit 416067aa70a0f5f9e675b61c65c99a1589d4f7e0
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Aman Jayesh (LICENSE)

The rewrite keeps what the plugin gave a user: one `routine` tool with the same
six actions, a routine stored as routine.json and routine.py with every saved
version beside them, the operator's approval bound to the sha256 of the exact
bytes (so an edit on disk revokes it and a new save asks again), the defaults a
routine keeps and the values one run lays over them, the app guard and the
prelude prepended to the script, the script fed to `python -m phone_harness.run`
on stdin, the `@@AUTOPILOT` events read back as checkpoint / step / guard / done,
a run recorded under runs/ with the checkpoint that missed, the screen it saw
and the path of the screenshot it left, the newest 50 runs kept, and the slash
command that lists, shows, runs, deletes and moves the app allowlist. The engine
is rewritten in Lisp; the runner is not bundled and not installed - the cell
runs the phone-harness copy the operator already has, as the plugin ran the
copy its host installed.

Left out: the live phone pane and the `::routine` card (Hermes's desktop
plugin UI, which Nodecode does not have; the tool's JSON and the run records
are what the pane showed), image refs for canvas apps (cropping a screenshot
with Pillow and matching it on the phone - a routine here can still take a
screenshot with phone-harness and compare it by hand), the time-spent ranking
the prelude's cost event fed (the events are kept; the ranking is not
computed), the daily automation blueprint with its cron prompt (Nodecode's
cron cell takes the prompt), the Ask-about-an-app card the pane answered (the
guard stops the run and names the app; `/phone-autopilot allow` answers),
Hermes's approval rule keys, plugin data folders and toolset selection, and the
model-facing skill, whose working method the tool's own description and the
help topic carry instead.
